  "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B039",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and experimental development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B024",
    "B044"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I retain B039 without changing the configuration. Optical lattice clocks rest on a distinct physical principle, the magic-wavelength lattice clock, and on experimental work that made this principle into a reproducible precision instrument. Their maturity is demonstrated by independent operation at extraordinary accuracy, not merely by projected applications. The Katori-Ye allocation remains unusually compact and complementary: it can recognize the foundational clock concept and decisive experimental development while excluding later incremental gains in performance. This is both a fundamental advance in controlling light-matter systems and a durable expansion of what can be measured.",
    "case_against": "The citation must not turn all subsequent clock improvements or all precision-timekeeping work into part of the prize. Even this relatively clean two-person case depends on a wider metrology community, and the final historical examination must establish the exact respective foundational roles of Katori and Ye. Other candidates may present a more direct discovery of a new physical phenomenon rather than an enabling measurement capability.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that circuit QED is a distinct, mature quantum-optical regime with a coherent Blais-Wallraff-Schoelkopf theory-to-experiment allocation, rather than simply a quantum-computing technology.",
        "response": "I accept that narrow framing and agree that B008 is a serious achievement in controllable quantum optics. Nonetheless, its historical boundary remains more entangled with the wider superconducting-qubit programme. B039 has an equally persuasive record of reproducibility, a more sharply identifiable core principle, and a two-person allocation whose scope can be stated without claiming the subsequent development of an entire platform."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson emphasizes that aberration-corrected transmission electron microscopy became a dependable atom-by-atom measurement capability with wide scientific consequences, while requiring close separation of concepts, realization, and later engineering.",
        "response": "This reinforces my view that transformative measurement inventions deserve full consideration; B044 remains an alternative. But the same attribution discipline favors B039 at present: the lattice-clock principle and its foundational realization can be delimited more compactly, whereas the microscopy case still requires a finer resolution of competing correction concepts, practical implementation, and broader instrument development."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson regards WMAP as a completed empirical transformation and cautions that the most striking fundamental tests enabled by lattice clocks are still developing.",
        "response": "WMAP's completed scientific impact is an important comparative standard. Yet the case for B039 does not depend on future tests: the clocks have already established a new, reproducible level of frequency measurement and a broadly usable experimental capability. A Nobel citation can properly recognize the completed invention and experimental development, while leaving particular future applications outside its scope."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical documentation can state Katori's and Ye's distinct foundational contributions with sufficient precision while excluding later performance advances.",
      "Whether the citation should refer only to the lattice-clock invention and development, rather than to particular metrological or fundamental-physics applications."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No specific personal, collaborative, or institutional connection to the proposed laureates is apparent from the supplied profile. My work in quantum optics and precision measurement gives relevant technical familiarity but does not itself create an identified conflict."
  }
}
